# L0 / B012 — CLAUDE.md and repository product/build files.

C R213 OL GOV-004 | REQUIRES_APPROVAL | If an omitted detail must be decided and materially affects observable product behavior, propose the smallest reasonable option and ask for approval; defining product behavior or tunable values the product sources do not define is Reserved. | product ambiguity | none stated
C R214 OL GOV-005 | DEFAULT | Routine internal implementation choices that do not change observable behavior do not require approval; do not ask approval for routine decisions already constrained by product sources, the approved stack, existing code or these rules. | internal implementation choices | none stated
C R215 OL GOV-010 | REQUIRES_APPROVAL | Do not silently invent a tunable value that materially changes accepted input or observable behavior: propose it with the reason, ask for approval, record it in CUSTOM_SETTINGS.md and synchronize every affected surface (custom-settings). | tunable values | none stated
C R216 OL GOV-011 | MUST NOT | Never store secrets, environment-specific connection details, package versions, HTTP status codes, ports, migration identifiers or incidental implementation constants in CUSTOM_SETTINGS.md. | CUSTOM_SETTINGS.md | none stated
C X27 EX - | MUST | Normative vocabulary: 'must / must not' violations are defects; a 'default' may be deviated from only with a concrete reason and the deviation must be reported; 'prefer / avoid' are guidance; 'requires approval' marks a Reserved decision. | interpretation of all instruction text | none stated | The oracle's DEFAULT strength allows deviation for a concrete reason but requires no report of each deviation; the reporting duty and the vocabulary itself are template constructs. | Choosing explicit transactions or a non-URL versioning strategy for a concrete reason must still be reported to the user.
C X28 EX GOV-017,GOV-005 | MUST | Unclear-case test: any 'yes' makes a choice Reserved — does it add, remove or replace a dependency, framework or infrastructure capability; change a project/layer/reference boundary; establish a convention other code is expected to follow; change observable behavior or a public contract; become expensive to reverse once other code depends on it? | choices whose classification is unclear | none stated | GOV-017 requires classification but not this test; under it any unclear dependency addition or removal becomes Reserved, whereas the oracle allows dependencies with a concrete need (DEP-001) and delegates routine internal choices (GOV-005). | An unclear-case addition of a small utility package must go through a Decision Request instead of add-dependency alone.
C X29 EX GOV-018 | MUST | Instruction precedence: engineering policy lives in the instruction system; a more volatile level may refine a more stable level but must not contradict it; when a contradiction is found it is reported instead of choosing a side. | conflicts between instruction levels | none stated | GOV-018 asks to raise concerns about questionable requests, including conflicts; no oracle policy defines precedence between instruction levels or requires reporting internal instruction contradictions instead of resolving them. | When a Level 2 rule contradicts a Level 1 rule the agent must report the contradiction rather than follow either one silently.
C X30 EX RUN-009,DEP-023 | MUST (repository build configuration) | Repository-wide Directory.Build.props enables nullable reference types, implicit usings and the latest analysis level for every .NET project. | all .NET projects | Project-specific overrides. | DEP-023 places shared settings in Directory.Build.props and DEP-008 fixes the platform; no oracle policy requires nullable reference types, implicit usings or the latest analyzer level. | Every project compiles with nullable analysis and the latest analyzers, which changes warnings and which using directives are redundant.

